1:["$","$Ld",null,{"item":{"id":"teach-the-picture","number":"04","title":"Explain how to read the figure.","mode":"Visual overload","scale":"slide","sourceType":"public","dek":"Before presenting a complicated figure, explain what its lines, regions, or symbols mean. Teach one element at a time, then show how they fit together.","point":"Reading a figure requires knowing what its visual elements represent. Introduce the axes and explain one curve, shaded region, or line style before asking the audience to interpret several together. Build toward the complete figure while keeping familiar elements in place, so each addition extends what listeners already understand.","source":{"authors":"Amanda E. Kowalski","title":"Estimating the Tradeoff Between Risk Protection and Moral Hazard with a Nonlinear Budget Set Model of Health Insurance","date":"October 2012","url":"https://users.nber.org/~kowalski/nlbspres10_25_12.pdf","pages":"16, 20"},"before":[{"kind":"image","title":"Identification diagram · PDF p. 16","alt":"Three panels connect a nonlinear contract, multiple linear budgets with indifference curves, and a demand diagram using projection lines and symbolic intercepts.","image":"/originals/kowalski-2012-p16.png","id":"identification","sourcePage":"16"},{"id":"numerical-budget","kind":"working","title":"The $350-deductible plan: budget segments","provenance":"Schematic reconstruction","formula":"A-B=\\begin{cases}-Q,&0\\le Q\\le350,\\\\-280-0.2Q,&350\\le Q\\le9{,}100,\\\\-2{,}100,&Q\\ge9{,}100.\\end{cases}","sourcePage":"16, 20","lines":["$$350 deductible · 20% coinsurance · $2,100 patient-payment cap","\\(Q\\): total medical spending · \\(A\\): other goods · \\(B\\): income after premium","Upper threshold: $350 + ($2,100 − $350) / 0.2 = $9,100."]},{"id":"linear-budgets","kind":"text","title":"Local linear budgets","provenance":"Schematic reconstruction","formula":"(p,y)=(1,B),\\ (0.2,B-280),\\ (0,B-2{,}100)","sourcePage":"16, 20","lines":["\\(A+pQ=y\\), where \\(p\\) is the price of care and \\(y\\) is virtual income.","Extend each straight segment to zero medical spending to obtain its intercept.","The middle segment has slope \\(-0.2\\), intercept \\(B-280\\), and actual range $350–$9,100."]}],"after":[{"kind":"insurance-linearization","stage":0,"title":"On this segment, another dollar of care costs the patient 20¢.","label":"THE COINSURANCE SEGMENT","alt":"The translated budget A minus B declines from zero to minus $350 at total spending $350, then at slope minus 0.2 to total spending $9,100 and other spending minus $2,100; it is flat after that. The coinsurance segment is highlighted.","revises":["numerical-budget"],"facts":[{"label":"Offered plan","text":"$$350 deductible · 20% coinsurance · $2,100 payment cap"}],"terms":[{"symbol":"A","meaning":"Spending on other goods"},{"symbol":"B","meaning":"Income after the insurance premium"}]},{"kind":"insurance-linearization","stage":1,"title":"Extending the segment reveals its virtual-income intercept.","label":"NEAR THE DEDUCTIBLE · $0–$2,000 OF CARE","formula":"A+0.2Q=B-280","alt":"The highlighted middle contract segment is extended with a dashed line to Q equals zero. Its intercept is minus $280 relative to B; the actual budget at zero spending has intercept zero.","revises":["linear-budgets","numerical-budget"],"terms":[{"symbol":"A","meaning":"Spending on other goods"},{"symbol":"B","meaning":"Income after the insurance premium"}]},{"kind":"insurance-linearization","stage":2,"title":"Each segment has its own price and virtual income.","label":"THREE LOCAL BUDGETS","formula":"A+pQ=y","revises":["linear-budgets","numerical-budget"],"terms":[{"symbol":"A","meaning":"Spending on other goods"},{"symbol":"B","meaning":"Income after the insurance premium"}]}],"diagnosis":"The identification figure combines budgets, curves, and projection lines. One coinsurance segment makes its visual grammar concrete: the negative slope is the marginal price; the extended intercept is virtual income.","changes":["Identify the actual contract segment.","Distinguish its linear extension from the actual contract.","Name the price and virtual income encoded by each segment."],"preserved":"The piecewise budget and its local price–income pairs, using a contract actually offered in the study.","deferred":"The full demand panel and identification argument stay in the source. Geometry alone does not establish identification.","practice":"Name what one solid line, dashed line, or projection means before combining them in a larger figure.","principle":"Let the slide unfold","context":"Actual research talk. The contract and model precede this figure. This repair teaches one operation inside the identification argument. PDF p. 15 states the identifying variation and functional-form assumptions; p. 20 supplies the offered-plan parameters.","beforeCaption":"The source diagram and the same numerical budget and local intercepts written out schematically.","afterCaption":"The same contract, with one geometric operation at a time.","values":["F=1, D=$350, C=0.2, S=$2,100.","The middle segment satisfies A−B=−350−0.2(Q−350)=−280−0.2Q.","At Q=$350 and Q=$9,100 the adjoining budgets agree.","The dashed line is a mathematical extension, not a contract outside the indicated range.","The three local budgets have different virtual incomes; do not join their choices into a single ordinary Marshallian demand curve without further assumptions."],"topic":"Health insurance"}}]
